<section class="alt">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-label">Why Docs, specifically</span>
    <h2>When a document beats a spreadsheet</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Built-in outline navigation</h3>
        <p>Every day is a real heading, so Google Docs' outline panel (View, then Show outline) turns into a clickable table of contents for the whole trip.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Room for context</h3>
        <p>Restaurant recommendations from a friend, a note about which entrance to use at a museum, why you picked this hotel. A document has room for the stuff a spreadsheet cell doesn't.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Comments, not just edits</h3>
        <p>If you're planning with someone else, Docs comments let them suggest changes to a specific day without touching the actual text, cleaner than a shared spreadsheet where edits just happen.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Print-friendly by default</h3>
        <p>File then Print produces a clean, readable itinerary without extra formatting work, useful for a physical copy at the airport when your phone's about to die.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-label">Who it's for</span>
    <h2>If you think in paragraphs, not rows</h2>
    <p>Good fit if you want to write your itinerary the way you'd explain it to a friend, with reasoning, not just data. If you'd rather track a budget alongside the schedule, or want everything as sortable rows, the <a href="/templates/itinerary-template-google-sheets/">Google Sheets version</a> is built for that instead. Same trip-planning logic, different format for a different way of working.</p>
